# L §8 What a result reads (tally 2.1.7 R1, issue #140; #116): `ties by (e1, e2)` compares e2 only among the
# candidates e1 leaves tied (L §5.11 l.1170). q1 and q2 tie on cost; rank decides (q2), so sub[q1], a hole, is never
# read. b: a tie-break term that is a hole where it is read gives the hole (the least of a hole is a hole), never a
# key. 2.1.6's refcore read every term for every tied key and answered the tied key itself: q1 for both.
=== m.tally
model p2b
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

index qs = [q1, q2]

input cost[k in qs]: int

input rank[k in qs]: int

input sub[k in qs]: int

ask a: argmin(cost[o] for o in qs) ties by (rank[o], sub[o])
ask b: argmin(cost[o] for o in qs) ties by (sub[o], rank[o])

version v1
  cite card
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  cost[q1] = 5 "card"
  cost[q2] = 5 "card"
  rank[q1] = 2 "card"
  rank[q2] = 1 "card"
  sub[q2] = 7 "card"
=== expect a b
a: q2
  cut 2026-03-01
b: ?"sub at q1: no value" (sub[q1])
  cut 2026-03-01
